Given a rational map $f : V \dashrightarrow W$ between projective varieties and $V$ is non-singular. Denote $I_f$ to be the indeterminacy locus of $f$ and $U:= V\backslash I_f$. Let $j: U \lhook\joinrel\longrightarrow V$ be the inclusion map and $f|_{U}:U\longrightarrow W$ be the restriction of $f$ on $U$, which is a morphism between quasi-projective varieties. Then $f|_{U}^* : \operatorname{Pic}(W)  \longrightarrow \operatorname{Pic}(U)$ is well-defined and we define $f^{-1}:=(j^*)^{-1} \circ f|_{U}^* : \operatorname{Pic}(W) \longrightarrow \operatorname{Pic}(V)$, i.e. $f^{-1}$ is defined such that the following diagram commutes:
$$\xymatrix{
&\operatorname{Pic}(U)  
&\operatorname{Pic}(W) \ar@{->}[l]_{f|_{U}^*} \ar@{->}[dl]^{f^{-1}} \\
&\operatorname{Pic}(V)  \ar@{->}[u]^{j^*}_\cong 
}$$

Since $f$ is a rational map between smooth projective varieties, the indeterminacy locus $I_f$ has codimension at least $2$, i.e. each irreducible component of $I_f$ has dimension at most $n-2$.

Define the quasi-projective varieties $U^o:=\mathbb{P}^n\backslash I_f$ and $\Gamma^o:=\Gamma\backslash I_f \cap \Gamma$ and we have the following commutative diagram 

$$\xymatrix{
\Gamma^o \ar@{^{(}->}[d]_{j_{\Gamma^o}} \ar@{^{(}->}[r]^{\iota_{\Gamma^o}}
&U^o \ar@{->}[r]^{f|_{U^o}} \ar@{^{(}->}[d]_{j_{U^o}}
&\mathbb{P}^r \\
\Gamma \ar@{^{(}->}[r]^{\iota_\Gamma} 
&\mathbb{P}^n \ar@{-->}[ur]_f}
$$
where all the $\iota$ and $j$ are inclusion maps and $f|_{U^o}$ is the restriction of $f$ on $U^o$. Since $\Gamma$ does not contain any codimension-two component of $I_f$, the codimension of $I_f \cap \Gamma$ in $\Gamma$ is at least $2$ also. Thus, we have the following isomorphisms of divisor class groups induced by $j_{\Gamma^o}$ and $j_{U^o}$:
\begin{align*} j^*_{U^o}: \operatorname{Pic}(\mathbb{P}^n) &\stackrel{\cong}{\longrightarrow} \operatorname{Pic}(U^o)  &j^*_{\Gamma^o}: \operatorname{Pic}(\Gamma) &\stackrel{\cong}{\longrightarrow} \operatorname{Pic}(\Gamma^o)\\
 c &\longmapsto c \cap U^o & D &\longmapsto D \cap \Gamma^o,
\end{align*} with projective closure in the corresponding varieties as the inverse maps. With these isomorphisms and the above commutative diagram, we obtain the following commutative diagram of divisor class groups:


$$\xymatrix{
\operatorname{Pic}(\Gamma^o)
&\operatorname{Pic}(U^o)  \ar@{->}[l]_{\iota_{\Gamma^o}^*} 
&\operatorname{Pic}(\mathbb{P}^r) \ar@{->}[l]_{f|_{U^o}^*} \ar@{->}[dl]^{f^{-1}} \\
\operatorname{Pic}(\Gamma) \ar@{->}[u]^{j_{\Gamma^o}^*}_\cong 
&\operatorname{Pic}(\mathbb{P}^n)  \ar@{->}[u]^{j_{U^o}^*}_\cong \ar@{->}[l]_{\iota_\Gamma^*} 
}$$

Pull-backs are functorial on the category of varieties (\cite{FIT} Chapter 2, Section 2.2, \cite{HAG} Appendix A, Theorem 1.1), so for any divisor class $c \in \operatorname{Pic}(\mathbb{P}^r)$, we have 
$$\iota_{\Gamma^o}^*(f|_{U^o}^*(c))=(f|_{U^o} \circ \iota_{\Gamma^o})^*(c). $$ Then by taking $(j_{\Gamma^o}^*)^{-1}$, the projective closure in $\Gamma$ and tracing the commutative diagram, we get
\begin{align*}
\iota_{\Gamma}^*(f^{-1}(c))&=(j_{\Gamma^o}^*)^{-1}\left(\iota_{\Gamma^o}^*(f|_{U^o}^*(c))\right) \\& = (j_{\Gamma^o}^*)^{-1}\left((f|_{U^o} \circ \iota_{\Gamma^o})^*(c)\right) \\ &= (f \circ \iota_\Gamma)^{-1}(c), \end{align*} which is the statement of the lemma.

Let $g_{P_\Gamma} : \Gamma \dashrightarrow \mathbb{P}^2$ be the rational map induced by $P_\Gamma=[x_\Gamma: y_\Gamma: z_\Gamma]$. Then $g_{P_\Gamma}$ factors through
$$\xymatrix{
\Gamma \ar@{-->}[r]^{g_{P_\Gamma}} \ar@{^{(}->}[d]_{\iota_{\Gamma}} 
&\mathbb{P}^2  \\
\mathbb{P}^n \ar@{-->}[ur]_{f_P} 
}$$
Let $L \in \operatorname{Pic}(\mathbb{P}^2)$ be the divisor class of hyperplanes, then we have 
\begin{align*}
h_{E_\Gamma}(P_\Gamma)&= \deg g_{P_\Gamma}^{-1}(L) & (\text{by Proposition \ref{LH}})\\
&=\deg (f_P \circ \iota_\Gamma)^{-1}(L)\\
&= \deg \iota_\Gamma^* (f_P^{-1}(L)) &(\text{by Lemma \ref{Le1}}).
\end{align*}
But the divisor class $\iota_\Gamma^* (f_P^{-1}(L))$ is the divisor class of the intersection of $\Gamma$ and $f_P^{-1}(L)$ (\cite{FIT} Chapter 8, Example 8.1.7 or \cite{HAG} Chapter II, Exercises 6.2). Then by B\'ezout's theorem, we get $\deg \iota_\Gamma^* (f_P^{-1}(L)) = \deg \Gamma \cdot \deg f_P^{-1}(L)$, which gives 
$$h_{E_\Gamma}(P_\Gamma)=\deg \Gamma \cdot \deg f_P^{-1}(L)=\deg \Gamma \cdot h_E(P) $$ by Proposition \ref{LH} again.

Let $t=[0,1:*:\ldots:*] \in H_\infty(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}) \backslash H'_\infty(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ such that the rational map $f_P$ is not defined at $t$. Then exactly one of the following statements is true:
\begin{itemize}
\item[a)] $f_{P'}$ is not defined at $t$,
\item[b)] $P'_t$ is a singular point on  $E'_t(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$,
\item[c)] $P'_t$ is a torsion point of order $2$ on $E'_t(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$.
\end{itemize}

Define the quasi-projective varieties $U^o:=\mathbb{P}^n\backslash I_f$ and $\Gamma^o:=\Gamma\backslash I_f \cap \Gamma$ and we have the following commutative diagram 

$$\xymatrix{
\Gamma^o \ar@{^{(}->}[d]_{j_{\Gamma^o}} \ar@{^{(}->}[r]^{\iota_{\Gamma^o}}
&U^o \ar@{->}[r]^{f|_{U^o}} \ar@{^{(}->}[d]_{j_{U^o}}
&\mathbb{P}^r \\
\Gamma \ar@{^{(}->}[r]^{\iota_\Gamma} 
&\mathbb{P}^n \ar@{-->}[ur]_f}
$$
where all the $\iota$ and $j$ are inclusion maps and $f|_{U^o}$ is the restriction of $f$ on $U^o$.

With these isomorphisms and the above commutative diagram, we obtain the following commutative diagram of divisor class groups:


$$\xymatrix{
\operatorname{Pic}(\Gamma^o)
&\operatorname{Pic}(U^o)  \ar@{->}[l]_{\iota_{\Gamma^o}^*} 
&\operatorname{Pic}(\mathbb{P}^r) \ar@{->}[l]_{f|_{U^o}^*} \ar@{->}[dl]^{f^{-1}} \\
\operatorname{Pic}(\Gamma) \ar@{->}[u]^{j_{\Gamma^o}^*}_\cong 
&\operatorname{Pic}(\mathbb{P}^n)  \ar@{->}[u]^{j_{U^o}^*}_\cong \ar@{->}[l]_{\iota_\Gamma^*} 
}$$

Let $g_{P_\Gamma} : \Gamma \dashrightarrow \mathbb{P}^2$ be the rational map induced by $P_\Gamma=[x_\Gamma: y_\Gamma: z_\Gamma]$. Then $g_{P_\Gamma}$ factors through
$$\xymatrix{
\Gamma \ar@{-->}[r]^{g_{P_\Gamma}} \ar@{^{(}->}[d]_{\iota_{\Gamma}} 
&\mathbb{P}^2  \\
\mathbb{P}^n \ar@{-->}[ur]_{f_P} 
}$$

Let $L \in \operatorname{Pic}(\mathbb{P}^2)$ be the divisor class of hyperplanes, then we have 
\begin{align*}
h_{E_\Gamma}(P_\Gamma)&= \deg g_{P_\Gamma}^{-1}(L) & (\text{by Proposition \ref{LH}})\\
&=\deg (f_P \circ \iota_\Gamma)^{-1}(L)\\
&= \deg \iota_\Gamma^* (f_P^{-1}(L)) &(\text{by Lemma \ref{Le1}}).
\end{align*}

To summarize, if $\Gamma 
eq H_\infty$ is a hyperplane not contained in the complement of $U$, does not contain subvarieties in $B_E \cup S_E$ and is not a component of the discriminant locus of $E$, then  egin{equation}\det \left( \<P^i_\Gamma, P^j_\Gamma\>_{E_\Gamma} ight)_{1\leq i,j\leq r} 
eq 0 \end{equation} and lemma ef{LNS} holds for $P^1_\Gamma, \ldots, P^r_\Gamma$. The set of such hyperplanes $\Gamma \subset \mathbb{P}^n$ is Zariski dense in the dual variety ${\mathbb{P}^n}^*$ (the set of hyperplanes in $\mathbb{P}^n$) because the conditions of not containing a finite number of subvarieties and not equaling any one of a certain finite number of hyperplanes are open conditions. Thus, there are infinitely many hyperplanes $\Gamma$ that allow us to reduce the dimension from $\mathbb{P}^n$ to $\mathbb{P}^{n-1}$. As mentioned before, although we may not have the minimality of Weierstrass equation in the $\mathbb{P}^{n-1}$ case, but we do know that lemma ef{LNS} holds for $P^1_\Gamma, \ldots, P^r_\Gamma$, which is enough for Theorem A to hold true for $P^1_\Gamma, \ldots, P^r_\Gamma$ on $E_\Gamma(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}(\Gamma))$ and allow us to apply inductively this reduction on the dimension of the projective space and eventually reduce to the case $\mathbb{P}^2$, i.e. we may assume $E$ is an elliptic curve defined over $K=\overline{\mathbb{Q}}(\mathbb{P}^2)$ with $P^1, \ldots, P^r \in E(K)$ such that $\det \left( \<P^i, P^j\>_{E} ight)_{1\leq i,j\leq r} 
eq 0$.

We continue to reduce the dimension to one as above but do not restrict ourselves to just hyperplanes, i.e. we can choose any smooth curve $C \subset \mathbb{P}^2$ of degree $d$ not contained in the complement of $U$, not a component of the discriminant locus of $E$ and does not contain subvarieties in $B_E$. The union of all such curves is the Zariski open set $\mathbb{P}^2\backslash B_E$, because given any point $t \in \mathbb{P}^2(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})\backslash B_E$, we can always find a smooth curve $C/\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of degree $d$ such that it contains $t$ but not points in $B_E$ and it is not a component of the discriminant locus of $E$ and $\mathbb{P}^2\backslash U$. With this construction, over these infinitely many curves $C$ of degree $d$, we have $\det \left( \<P^i_C, P^j_C\>_{E_C} \right)_{1\leq i,j\leq r} \neq 0$. By continuity of the determinant function and Silverman's theorem (\cite{SHS}, Theorem B), we conclude that 
$\det \left( \<{P^i_C}_t, {P^j_C}_t\>_{{E_C}_t} \right)_{1\leq i,j\leq r} \neq 0,$ for all $t \in C(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ with large enough $h_C(t)$. This means that the specialized points ${P^1_C}_t, \ldots, {P^r_C}_t$ are $\mathbb{Z}$-linearly independent. Since we always choose the hyperplanes (or smooth curves in the last induction step) not being contained in the complement of $U$, the specialization maps for $t \in U(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ commute with reduction maps as shown in Figure 1.
\begin{figure}
$$\xymatrix{
E(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}(\mathbb{P}^n)) \ar@{->}[r]^{\sigma_t} \ar@{->}[d]_{\text{red mod $\Gamma$}} 
&E_t(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})  \\
E_\Gamma((\overline{\mathbb{Q}}(\Gamma))) \ar@{->}[d] & \\
\vdots \ar@{->}[d]_{\text{red mod $C$}} & \\
E_C(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}(C)) \ar@{->}[uuur]_{\sigma_t} 
}$$
\caption{}
\end{figure}
So for $t \in C(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}) \cap U(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ with $h_C(t)$ large enough, we have injectivity of $\sigma_t$ on both the free part and the torsion points of $E(K)$.
